16
00:01:21,921 --> 00:01:27,960
Manhattan is an island, and the fight against water here is an everyday reality for its

17
00:01:27,961 --> 00:01:33,620
1.6 million residents.

18
00:01:33,621 --> 00:01:42,640
But it's 140 feet beneath these crowded streets that the real war against water rages on.

19
00:01:42,641 --> 00:01:46,800
In New York City's subway, water that seeps into tunnels has always been the number one

20
00:01:46,801 --> 00:01:47,800
enemy.

21
00:01:47,801 --> 00:01:51,920
We'll stop working on a Saturday morning, and by the time we return to work on a Monday,

22
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:55,960
we'll have two to three, four feet of water in here, and that's without rain.

23
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:02,120
The Metropolitan Transportation Authority is halfway through a massive subway expansion project.

24
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:09,220
We have anywhere from six to eight of these bumps constantly running to retrieve the water,

25
00:02:09,221 --> 00:02:11,900
and we put it through a pump out into the sewer lines.

26
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:17,460
They've dug out two new colossal tunnels down here.

27
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:23,000
And in doing so, they put themselves under a watery siege, 24-7.

28
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:33,620
All transit systems that are underground primarily, we are every day pumping 13 million gallons of water on a dry day.

29
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:37,840
If you're underground, you have force to reckon with, and that's hydrostatic pressure.

30
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:42,440
The mass of water and the mass of Earth puts water under pressure.

31
00:02:42,441 --> 00:02:47,440
If you're going to build a hole in the ground like a tunnel, then water in the ground around

32
00:02:47,441 --> 00:02:53,420
