39
00:03:21,921 --> 00:03:28,840
Strasser introduced this ingenious waterproofing system to America in 1984.

40
00:03:28,841 --> 00:03:34,440
Since then, they've done installations in over 65 underground structures in the U.S.,

41
00:03:34,441 --> 00:03:37,280
including stations on the Washington, D.C. metro.

42
00:03:37,281 --> 00:03:42,280
But can Wisco really keep water out of the New York subway?

43
00:03:42,281 --> 00:03:46,280
Leaks have plagued the system since the beginning.

44
00:03:46,281 --> 00:03:50,940
New York first began building a subway in 1900.

45
00:03:50,941 --> 00:03:56,380
The battle against drips and leaks started at the same time.

46
00:03:56,381 --> 00:03:59,500
Builders tried using asphalt to keep leaks out.

47
00:03:59,501 --> 00:04:07,740
New York City has a lot of subway systems built in the 30s and 40s, you know.

48
00:04:07,741 --> 00:04:13,620
The tunnels are leaking, all the tiles falling down, because at that time they didn't have

49
00:04:13,621 --> 00:04:18,180
the right technique to waterproof a tunnel.

50
00:04:18,181 --> 00:04:23,180
Leakage in the subways has cost millions of dollars in damage, in the form of rusty tracks,

51
00:04:23,181 --> 00:04:27,340
