146
00:11:26,901 --> 00:11:29,920
Dribble's sub could dive down 12 to 15 feet.

147
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:33,800
Waterproofing technology made great strides,

148
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:35,820
and by the time World War II ended,

149
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,860
submarines could dive down to about 400 feet.

150
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:41,640
It was dangerous work.

151
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:46,020
20% of Americans who fought in submarines never returned.

152
00:11:47,860 --> 00:11:50,880
Today, thanks to advanced waterproofing methods,

153
00:11:51,660 --> 00:11:54,660
submersibles can explore ocean depths never seen before.

154
00:11:56,900 --> 00:12:03,900
The ALVAN, operated by the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institute, can descend to a depth of three

155
00:12:03,901 --> 00:12:04,900
miles.

156
00:12:04,901 --> 00:12:11,980
That's as deep as 12 Empire State Buildings stacked top to bottom.

157
00:12:11,981 --> 00:12:16,400
No matter how deep it goes, a submarine's ability to withstand water pressure starts

158
00:12:16,401 --> 00:12:21,340
with exceptionally strong seals along every opening.

159
00:12:21,341 --> 00:12:27,180
These are the most waterproof and the most pressure proof of all the seals are for the

160
00:12:27,181 --> 00:12:28,180
motors.

161
00:12:28,181 --> 00:12:32,100
These are actually stainless steel shafts that penetrate the hull, and within that shaft

162
00:12:32,101 --> 00:12:36,780
