163
00:12:36,781 --> 00:12:41,620
The hatch to the conning tower, which allows the operator in and out, is the largest opening

164
00:12:41,621 --> 00:12:44,260
that needs to be sealed.

165
00:12:44,261 --> 00:12:49,380
This is the most important seal, really, because this seals you in with big bolting mechanisms

166
00:12:49,381 --> 00:12:51,094
inside that keeps the water out.

167
00:12:51,095 --> 00:12:53,880
The hatch is actually a little bit over quarter inch

168
00:12:53,881 --> 00:12:54,581
thick.

169
00:12:54,582 --> 00:12:59,900
We've got an O-ring seal in here made out of rubber put into a specially lathe groove.

170
00:12:59,901 --> 00:13:05,120
As the pressure goes down on it, it'll push this O-ring into a little cavity, especially

171
00:13:05,121 --> 00:13:07,063
designed for this to seal into.

172
00:13:07,064 --> 00:13:10,760
Because you've got literally hundreds of pounds of pressure

173
00:13:10,761 --> 00:13:14,620
pushing down on this. At 10 feet deep, you wouldn't be able to open this hatch, just

174
00:13:14,621 --> 00:13:17,940
with water pressure on it. Well, let's go over the seals.

175
00:13:17,941 --> 00:13:22,980
Ironically, submarines do let in water in order to submerge.

